00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:20.100
I work a lot with our
own corp eng department.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.050
And obviously we need control.

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:23.040
We need access.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:25.060
We can't just let
data flow willy nilly.

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:27.540
We need to know where our
data is being accessed from,

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:28.910
how it's being accessed.

00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:29.910
It's about security.

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:30.826
It's about management.

00:01:30.826 --> 00:01:32.010
It's about control.

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:35.370
Today everything I talk about
is our general principles

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:37.470
of how we manage our endpoints.

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:40.740
So yes, I come from a
phone and tablet side,

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:43.770
but we're including laptops in
there because what we're seeing

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:46.410
is that while laptops and
desktops have been around

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:48.540
for much longer,
and there's always

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:51.180
been ways to update and
fix them with patching

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:54.990
and with things like big fix the
images and flashing, what we're

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:57.480
seeing is that the modern way
of doing management, which

00:01:57.480 --> 00:01:59.670
is through a set
of management APIs,

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:01.230
through strict
data of separation,

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:04.080
through security by default,
through rigorous app

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:06.000
stores for app
deployment, that's

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.070
the way we see endpoint
management going

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:10.169
across all endpoints,
all the way

00:02:10.169 --> 00:02:12.900
through certainly
mobile and tablets

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:14.640
and through laptops
and desktops,

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.850
but even all the way through
to IOT, the internet of things.

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:19.740
So this isn't the IOT talk.

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:20.700
I also cover that.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:23.786
I'm happy to answer questions
about that if folks have them.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.070
Why do we really need this?

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:32.520
Well, a lot of things have
changed in the last few years.

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:36.810
Since the upcoming advent of
mobile, a lot of new groups

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:38.580
have sprung up, a lot
of new technologies.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:40.870
A lot of new vendors
have come to us and said,

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:43.500
hey, we do device management,
and this is how you go.

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:46.200
A lot of you, your EMM or
MVM will be coming to you

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:48.060
and saying, well, I
used to do just mobile,

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:49.440
but I want to do your laptops.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:51.000
I want to do all
of your endpoints

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:54.720
as we move to a UEM, unified
endpoint management world.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:56.700
So all this won't
be new to you folks.

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:58.920
We're trying to manage
a lot of this complexity

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:02.300
and say, how do we have a
principle that overrides

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:05.010
and runs through all of
our various endpoints

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:08.520
so we're not solving things
in an individual way?

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:12.090
And something I always
said is that mobile is not

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:13.230
its own thing.

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:15.384
Mobile was its own thing
for the last few years.

00:03:15.384 --> 00:03:16.800
We have mobile
groups spinning up.

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.350
We have mobile application
developers, mobile device

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:20.550
management vendors.

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:23.850
But really, mobile is
kind of like what internet

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:26.370
was in the early 2000s.

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:29.460
In the early 2000s, late '90s,
the internet was very new.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:32.630
A lot of companies said, well,
we need an internet division

00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:33.697
and an internet group.

00:03:33.697 --> 00:03:35.280
And I'm going to
have an internet part

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:37.290
of my company, an
internet set of people

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:38.580
who work on that stuff.

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:41.370
But what happened was
everything is internet, right?

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:45.295
Internet is interwoven into the
fabric of all of our companies.

00:03:45.295 --> 00:03:46.920
And that's the same
thing about mobile.

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:50.040
Mobile is more just about
the proliferation of devices,

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:53.940
the proliferation of access,
the proliferation of networks

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:56.730
onto cellular networks,
which breaks down

00:03:56.730 --> 00:04:00.420
the old models of how we
used to do things in IT.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:02.430
So one of the reasons
I give this talk,

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:05.070
and I was introducing myself
as a product director,

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:09.150
I ran Android for Work, which is
part of the initiative to bring

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:10.920
Android into the enterprise.

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:13.105
But I also ran a mobile
application security

00:04:13.105 --> 00:04:16.089
and development at Morgan
Stanley for over five years.

00:04:16.089 --> 00:04:17.579
So I've sat on
both sides of this.

00:04:17.579 --> 00:04:19.560
And so I think very
carefully about what

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:21.600
it's like to be
within an organization

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:23.490
and trying to manage
this complexity,

00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:26.370
and saying what I
would do if I were you

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:28.770
folks in the modern world.

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:31.229
So how do we do
things at Google,

00:04:31.229 --> 00:04:32.770
and why is it a
little bit different?

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:36.360
First of all, we definitely
have an open culture.

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:39.780
And this open culture
means that we tend

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:42.669
to lean towards failing open.

00:04:42.669 --> 00:04:44.460
That means that we
don't have this idea of,

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:47.040
OK, well, I have my walls,
I have my buildings,

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:48.000
I have my internet.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:51.240
I'm going to go ahead
and lock into that

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:54.420
and manage everything
through those gardens.

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:57.900
Who here is familiar with
our BeyondCorp initiative

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:00.610
and has read some
of those papers?

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:02.270
OK, great.

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:04.360
What I would recommend
for everybody is that you

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:06.400
familiarize
yourself, BeyondCorp,

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:08.237
C-O-R-P is what
you'll want to Google,

00:05:08.237 --> 00:05:09.320
look around for materials.

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:11.620
I think we have a link at the
end of the deck for it as well.

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:13.578
BeyondCopr is something
that I would absolutely

00:05:13.578 --> 00:05:16.160
familiarize yourself with
if I was working today,

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:21.010
which is Google's
VPN-less intranet-less way

00:05:21.010 --> 00:05:23.290
of approaching how we
deploy applications.

00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:24.520
Yes, we are cloud centric.

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:25.600
Yes, we are cloud native.

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:26.890
We have a ton of advantages.

00:05:26.890 --> 00:05:31.540
But the principles apply to
every company, which is really

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:33.520
the idea of being
secure and safe

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:35.590
and managed because
you're on your intranet,

00:05:35.590 --> 00:05:37.630
those days are
rapidly going behind.

00:05:37.630 --> 00:05:38.950
That's an old model.

00:05:38.950 --> 00:05:41.620
It's reaching the end of its
lifetime is what we believe.

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:44.830
So here at Google, we
actually do have an intranet.

00:05:44.830 --> 00:05:47.950
We have Google A, for
example, for our Wi-Fi,

00:05:47.950 --> 00:05:49.200
which is an intranet.

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:51.430
And we have Google Guest.

00:05:51.430 --> 00:05:54.660
But I will say
that on my laptop,

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:58.010
I can't remember the last time
I needed to be on my intranet

00:05:58.010 --> 00:05:59.977
to access anything special.

00:05:59.977 --> 00:06:02.560
Oftentimes if I'm like, oh, I've
got a little bit of a problem

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:04.559
on Google A, I'll just
jump over to Google Guest

00:06:04.559 --> 00:06:05.890
and keep right on working.

00:06:05.890 --> 00:06:07.067
I don't re-fire up a VPN.

00:06:07.067 --> 00:06:07.900
I don't do anything.

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:09.580
I just keep on going.

00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:11.380
I can bring my laptop
from the intranet,

00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:13.360
pick it up, take it home.

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:14.740
Once again, no VPN, nothing.

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:17.477
All the security is done
through HTTP, through certs.

00:06:17.477 --> 00:06:19.060
I'll talk more about
that in a minute.

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:20.996
But the idea is that's
the experience we're

00:06:20.996 --> 00:06:22.120
trying to give to Googlers.

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:25.676
It's not, well, where am I,
and what network am I on,

00:06:25.676 --> 00:06:26.800
and how do I access things?

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:29.530
We try to make all of that go
away so people could just get

00:06:29.530 --> 00:06:32.500
their work done immediately.

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:36.070
So we fail to this idea of
really good user experiences

00:06:36.070 --> 00:06:39.460
and are willing to really
push the boundaries of moving

00:06:39.460 --> 00:06:42.400
past the traditional
comfort zones of IT

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:44.530
to make sure that when
people are able to work

00:06:44.530 --> 00:06:47.140
in this way, of course
security is paramount to us,

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:50.680
but we are up for doing the
extra engineering we need

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:53.530
to make sure we have
security and we have access.

00:06:53.530 --> 00:06:56.110
So just talking about
that, we're very aware.

00:06:56.110 --> 00:06:56.950
We're Google.

00:06:56.950 --> 00:06:59.050
We have Gmail.

00:06:59.050 --> 00:07:01.780
When people look at how people
are getting hacked and stuff

00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:05.180
about phishing, we take
security extremely, extremely,

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:06.191
extremely seriously.

00:07:06.191 --> 00:07:08.440
This is a theme of Next, and
hopefully all of you guys

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:09.850
are getting it.

00:07:09.850 --> 00:07:12.070
We are very aware
that professionals

00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:13.120
are being targeted.

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:14.530
I come from Morgan Stanley.

00:07:14.530 --> 00:07:18.340
I know from financial services,
very high value targets

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:20.020
for phishing, for
social engineering,

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:22.420
for people trying to
get in with malware.

00:07:22.420 --> 00:07:24.310
Very aware of all
of these targets.

00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:26.710
So security throughout
this talk is

00:07:26.710 --> 00:07:30.660
absolutely paramount to
everything that we do.

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:33.400
We have a group internally
for analysts, for detection,

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:36.670
for response, for prevention
on all of our endpoints.

00:07:36.670 --> 00:07:38.230
So when I tell you
something like,

00:07:38.230 --> 00:07:39.994
hey, we don't need
to have an intranet,

00:07:39.994 --> 00:07:41.410
it's not because
we're just taking

00:07:41.410 --> 00:07:42.826
and throwing
security to the wind,

00:07:42.826 --> 00:07:46.760
it's because we have a new way
of approaching these problems.

00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:49.080
And by the way, I'll have
room for questions at the end

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:50.060
where you'll be to ask them.

00:07:50.060 --> 00:07:51.640
But if you want to just stick
up your hand at any point,

00:07:51.640 --> 00:07:54.370
I'm happy to take questions
throughout the presentation.

00:07:54.370 --> 00:07:55.670
So this is Urs.

00:07:55.670 --> 00:07:57.380
You might have heard
him speak again.

00:07:57.380 --> 00:07:59.650
Urs runs our technical
infrastructure team.

00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:02.470
And he also owns the IT and
internal Corporate Engineering

00:08:02.470 --> 00:08:05.260
department, which we
refer to as corp eng.

00:08:05.260 --> 00:08:07.720
And we, I think, often say this.

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:10.279
And when I worked in IT, we
would say this all the time.

00:08:10.279 --> 00:08:12.070
But I want to refocus
everyone in the room,

00:08:12.070 --> 00:08:13.778
because I think this
is something we want

00:08:13.778 --> 00:08:15.250
to keep in front of our minds.

00:08:15.250 --> 00:08:19.000
IT and security is an enabler.

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:22.120
It should not be something
that restricts what you can do.

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:26.080
Security is what
enables you to do stuff.

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:28.810
In your personal lives,
when you pay for something

00:08:28.810 --> 00:08:33.309
with your credit card,
you are aware that this

00:08:33.309 --> 00:08:35.830
feels like a generally
secure way to pay, right?

00:08:35.830 --> 00:08:38.110
Like even though there is
credit card fraud out there,

00:08:38.110 --> 00:08:40.512
you're not dealing with
fraud on a day to day basis.

00:08:40.512 --> 00:08:42.220
Oh my god, I can't
pay at this restaurant

00:08:42.220 --> 00:08:44.178
because they might steal
my credit card number.

00:08:44.178 --> 00:08:45.595
Hopefully you're not.

00:08:45.595 --> 00:08:47.740
And because you have
that sense of security,

00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:49.200
and because of
all that work that

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:52.510
has gone into the
security of payments,

00:08:52.510 --> 00:08:56.220
you feel very comfortable
using your credit card

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:58.420
in a lot of different scenarios.

00:08:58.420 --> 00:09:00.190
So that's the kind
of feeling you

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:03.430
want to have as you work through
and as your users want to have,

00:09:03.430 --> 00:09:05.380
is that they know
they're secure,

00:09:05.380 --> 00:09:07.870
so they get this freedom
and this liberation

00:09:07.870 --> 00:09:09.920
to actually do more.

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:14.204
Too often security ends up
just being a gate or a blocker.

00:09:14.204 --> 00:09:16.120
No offense to the security
people in the room,

00:09:16.120 --> 00:09:18.190
but oftentimes when
we talk to customers,

00:09:18.190 --> 00:09:20.860
we talk to the IT side,
they say, oh, well, we'll

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:22.960
have to get this through
our security department.

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:26.170
And what we try to say is, let's
try to get to a relationship

00:09:26.170 --> 00:09:28.390
where security in
IT are collaborating

00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:31.690
to say how do we explore the
art of the possible while

00:09:31.690 --> 00:09:34.390
still keeping it
absolutely secure.

00:09:34.390 --> 00:09:36.340
So these are the
three dimensions

00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:37.420
of what we really feel.

00:09:37.420 --> 00:09:38.836
On the one hand,
there's security.

00:09:38.836 --> 00:09:40.660
IT security,
information security,

00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:43.714
you may have the same department
who does both of those things.

00:09:43.714 --> 00:09:45.130
It might be a
different department

00:09:45.130 --> 00:09:47.740
who does info sec from sec ops.

00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:50.170
Definitely there's usability,
and I talked about that.

00:09:50.170 --> 00:09:54.040
I think we all intuitively
know what usability is.

00:09:54.040 --> 00:09:56.050
And there's also privacy.

00:09:56.050 --> 00:09:58.510
And this is a really
important part of the puzzle

00:09:58.510 --> 00:10:02.116
as well, because if you
share all of your information

00:10:02.116 --> 00:10:03.490
all of the time,
and you actually

00:10:03.490 --> 00:10:06.610
surrender all of your
privacy as an individual,

00:10:06.610 --> 00:10:09.810
it actually makes the security
person's job a lot easier,

00:10:09.810 --> 00:10:11.560
to be honest.

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:14.170
But that's not necessarily
what employees want to do.

00:10:14.170 --> 00:10:17.110
And that's something we need
to take into account as well.

00:10:17.110 --> 00:10:19.900
Even though we all have
the big terms of services

00:10:19.900 --> 00:10:22.564
that can flash up and say, you
have no expectation of privacy,

00:10:22.564 --> 00:10:23.980
we can access all
your information

00:10:23.980 --> 00:10:25.480
while you're on net.

00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:29.200
Yes, of course, the company
can do all those things.

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:32.620
The company shouldn't
necessarily do all those things

00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:33.855
unless it really has to.

00:10:33.855 --> 00:10:35.980
So these are the three
pillars that we think about,

00:10:35.980 --> 00:10:39.010
security, usability and privacy.

00:10:39.010 --> 00:10:42.520
And while we recognize that it's
always a little bit of a push

00:10:42.520 --> 00:10:45.520
and pull in each direction,
part of the art and the science

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:48.220
here is finding a way to
find the right blend of all

00:10:48.220 --> 00:10:50.950
those things so that you
can have a wonderful access

00:10:50.950 --> 00:10:54.280
strategy internally, one
that you're excited about.

00:10:58.230 --> 00:11:01.920
This is part of what we
were going to go here.

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:05.820
I worked on mobile in the
sort of pure BlackBerry days,

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:07.860
the days when it was
like, well, that's it.

00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:10.290
BlackBerry did a great
job solving this problem.

00:11:10.290 --> 00:11:12.390
We're going to deploy
our BlackBerrys.

00:11:12.390 --> 00:11:14.700
It's going to be BlackBerry
through and through.

00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:16.382
We're going to run
the BES server.

00:11:16.382 --> 00:11:18.090
We're going to just
give out BlackBerrys.

00:11:18.090 --> 00:11:20.070
All good.

00:11:20.070 --> 00:11:22.950
And that was on the
one hand liberating,

00:11:22.950 --> 00:11:25.290
because this was a mobile
strategy for the first time

00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:27.770
that IT could take and say,
wow, we agree this is secure.

00:11:27.770 --> 00:11:28.590
This is great.

00:11:28.590 --> 00:11:29.280
Let's deploy it.

00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:32.460
Let's give mobile access to
all of these employees who

00:11:32.460 --> 00:11:33.930
never had it.

00:11:33.930 --> 00:11:37.440
On the other hand, it
was semi constraining.

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:41.130
Because what actually was
happening was all the strategy

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:43.020
was being deferred
to effectively one

00:11:43.020 --> 00:11:46.320
company, RIM, Research in
Motion, who makes BlackBerry.

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:49.050
And so when you do
that, basically you're

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:51.720
outsourcing your strategy
to a vendor in that case.

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:53.520
You're like, my
strategy is BlackBerry.

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:56.020
Which is kind of a strategy,
but actually what you're saying

00:11:56.020 --> 00:11:58.080
is my strategy is
BlackBerry's strategy,

00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:00.110
and they go hand-in-hand.

00:12:00.110 --> 00:12:03.090
And what happened
was is that, great,

00:12:03.090 --> 00:12:05.340
yes to everything BlackBerry,
no to everything else.

00:12:05.340 --> 00:12:07.131
And that was fine
because BlackBerry really

00:12:07.131 --> 00:12:08.370
was a very good device.

00:12:08.370 --> 00:12:11.040
When the iPhone came
around, that kind of blew up

00:12:11.040 --> 00:12:11.916
that model a lot.

00:12:11.916 --> 00:12:14.040
You couldn't really go to
the business stakeholders

00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:15.425
and say, oh, well,
you know what?

00:12:15.425 --> 00:12:16.800
It's just BlackBerry,
BlackBerry.

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:17.910
Some people still can.

00:12:17.910 --> 00:12:21.336
I know of customers who are
still in that world even today.

00:12:21.336 --> 00:12:22.710
But most companies
have now moved

00:12:22.710 --> 00:12:23.850
to a world where they're
like, well, listen,

00:12:23.850 --> 00:12:26.280
this is not the way we can
run our strategy anymore.

00:12:26.280 --> 00:12:28.432
We have to be able
to at least do iOS.

00:12:28.432 --> 00:12:30.390
And a lot of companies
just say, you know what?

00:12:30.390 --> 00:12:33.480
For BYOD, we're going to support
iOS and flavors of Android.

00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:35.460
This is how we want
to run our strategy.

00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:40.650
So really, you need to embrace
mobility and embrace access

00:12:40.650 --> 00:12:43.920
and feel comfortable that you
are covered in all regards,

00:12:43.920 --> 00:12:46.200
and not just say
this is my strategy.

00:12:46.200 --> 00:12:47.850
It's restricted
devices and that's it.

00:12:47.850 --> 00:12:50.909
Because even if you can find
a good middle ground today

00:12:50.909 --> 00:12:52.950
and say, you know what,
it's not just BlackBerry,

00:12:52.950 --> 00:12:55.470
it's BlackBerry and
iPhone, technology

00:12:55.470 --> 00:12:56.820
keeps on rolling forward.

00:12:56.820 --> 00:12:59.220
There'll soon be another
killer device, another killer

00:12:59.220 --> 00:13:01.270
platform, another way
of approaching it.

00:13:01.270 --> 00:13:02.940
How are you going
to do IOT sensors?

00:13:02.940 --> 00:13:06.660
How are you going to do ML
when people want to access data

00:13:06.660 --> 00:13:09.600
from their assistant units which
are sitting in their Google

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:10.390
homes?

00:13:10.390 --> 00:13:12.890
Technology keeps pushing and
pushing and pushing and pushing

00:13:12.890 --> 00:13:16.460
us to be able to have more
access for our company in more

00:13:16.460 --> 00:13:18.572
esoteric places all the time.

00:13:18.572 --> 00:13:21.030
I would not say that the answer
is not to just sort of say,

00:13:21.030 --> 00:13:22.902
well, it's this, then
it's this plus this,

00:13:22.902 --> 00:13:24.360
then it's this plus
this plus this.

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:26.360
You want a strategy that
embraces and allows you

00:13:26.360 --> 00:13:30.490
to approach everything at once.

00:13:30.490 --> 00:13:33.077
So how do we think
about access control?

00:13:33.077 --> 00:13:35.410
We break everything down and
we say, well, first of all,

00:13:35.410 --> 00:13:37.451
let's make sure we have
a really good idea of how

00:13:37.451 --> 00:13:39.040
we think of our people.

00:13:39.040 --> 00:13:42.570
This is going to be your
identity strategy, your IAM

00:13:42.570 --> 00:13:44.370
strategy.

00:13:44.370 --> 00:13:47.790
Devices, which is endpoints and
the state of those endpoints

00:13:47.790 --> 00:13:49.470
and how they're
managed and what data

00:13:49.470 --> 00:13:51.000
is going to those endpoints.

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:53.247
And this is the one that
people often overlook,

00:13:53.247 --> 00:13:54.705
even though it's
the most important

00:13:54.705 --> 00:13:58.750
and probably should be the
most obvious one, applications.

00:13:58.750 --> 00:14:00.960
How do we think
of and categorize

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:02.730
our various
applications, so we can

00:14:02.730 --> 00:14:05.621
decide what's appropriate
to access at any given time

00:14:05.621 --> 00:14:06.120
and where?

00:14:09.910 --> 00:14:11.940
On device management,
we actually

00:14:11.940 --> 00:14:14.400
centralized all
these concepts so

00:14:14.400 --> 00:14:16.620
that we are able
to control and have

00:14:16.620 --> 00:14:20.540
knobs to say this is what can
be accessed from where on which

00:14:20.540 --> 00:14:21.490
data device.

00:14:21.490 --> 00:14:27.110
So again, we move away from the
monolithic concept of on net,

00:14:27.110 --> 00:14:32.130
off net, on VPN, off VPN,
on mobile, off mobile.

00:14:32.130 --> 00:14:34.020
Too many of those
variables makes

00:14:34.020 --> 00:14:35.970
it impossible to
solve the equation.

00:14:35.970 --> 00:14:40.740
What we instead said is, let's
identify general security

00:14:40.740 --> 00:14:43.089
and access postures, tiers.

00:14:43.089 --> 00:14:44.130
We call it tiered access.

00:14:44.130 --> 00:14:45.830
I have a slide on
that coming up.

00:14:45.830 --> 00:14:48.580
How do we generalize it
into those categories?

00:14:48.580 --> 00:14:50.340
How do we know
which applications

00:14:50.340 --> 00:14:52.230
go into which category?

00:14:52.230 --> 00:14:55.830
And then let's just define
in any given moment in time

00:14:55.830 --> 00:15:00.180
which state a given endpoint
and user combination is in.

00:15:00.180 --> 00:15:01.710
And are they in a
good enough state

00:15:01.710 --> 00:15:04.870
that we're willing to give
access to a given application?

00:15:04.870 --> 00:15:07.300
It's actually pretty simple
when I describe it that way,

00:15:07.300 --> 00:15:12.810
but that's just a general
mantra that we go through.

00:15:12.810 --> 00:15:15.900
On the people level,
we want to make sure

00:15:15.900 --> 00:15:19.860
that a given identity has access
to the right applications.

00:15:19.860 --> 00:15:22.614
So this is fairly
simple stuff, but you've

00:15:22.614 --> 00:15:24.030
got to make sure
that you actually

00:15:24.030 --> 00:15:26.730
have this deployed properly
in your environment, which

00:15:26.730 --> 00:15:29.190
is maybe you're in HR.

00:15:29.190 --> 00:15:31.770
And so you should therefore
be able to say, OK, well

00:15:31.770 --> 00:15:33.660
I should be able to
see the actual employee

00:15:33.660 --> 00:15:35.230
records of a whole bunch
of different people.

00:15:35.230 --> 00:15:37.271
I should be to go in and
log into the application

00:15:37.271 --> 00:15:38.570
and see those employee records.

00:15:38.570 --> 00:15:39.569
OK, fantastic.

00:15:39.569 --> 00:15:41.610
Maybe you should only see
it for your department.

00:15:41.610 --> 00:15:44.200
Maybe you shouldn't see it
for the entire organization.

00:15:44.200 --> 00:15:45.840
Maybe you're not in
HR, you shouldn't

00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:47.631
be able to see those
records unless you are

00:15:47.631 --> 00:15:49.109
the manager of those people.

00:15:49.109 --> 00:15:51.150
Most of your companies
should have these concepts

00:15:51.150 --> 00:15:51.859
already in place.

00:15:51.859 --> 00:15:53.358
I'm just making
sure that we have it

00:15:53.358 --> 00:15:54.600
for the sake of completeness.

00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:56.880
But for HR, you might
have those in place,

00:15:56.880 --> 00:15:59.877
but you might not have
those in other applications.

00:15:59.877 --> 00:16:02.460
The idea is for HR, you're like,
well, that makes total sense.

00:16:02.460 --> 00:16:03.780
We should do it for payroll.

00:16:03.780 --> 00:16:05.280
Well yeah, I'm going
to do it there.

00:16:05.280 --> 00:16:06.340
That makes total sense.

00:16:06.340 --> 00:16:09.650
But you've to go through your
population of applications

00:16:09.650 --> 00:16:11.730
and make sure that
for each application,

00:16:11.730 --> 00:16:17.141
you have an idea of who should
be able to access it and when.

00:16:17.141 --> 00:16:19.929
On the devices standpoint--

00:16:19.929 --> 00:16:21.970
so we did all that for
our internal applications.

00:16:21.970 --> 00:16:24.320
On the devices standpoint
here at Google,

00:16:24.320 --> 00:16:26.090
just because people
ask these questions,

00:16:26.090 --> 00:16:29.184
we support a variety
of different devices.

00:16:29.184 --> 00:16:29.975
We support Android.

00:16:29.975 --> 00:16:33.020
And on Android, we support our
pixel devices and any Nexus

00:16:33.020 --> 00:16:37.050
device at the highest
tier of access.

00:16:37.050 --> 00:16:40.760
We also support Chrome OS at
the highest tier of access.

00:16:40.760 --> 00:16:42.901
And we support
Mac OS and Windows

00:16:42.901 --> 00:16:44.150
at our highest tier of access.

00:16:44.150 --> 00:16:46.290
So we actually support
our entire population.

00:16:46.290 --> 00:16:47.861
When a Googler
comes in, you might

00:16:47.861 --> 00:16:49.860
think that every Googler
gets an Android device.

00:16:49.860 --> 00:16:51.401
But you'll see that
a lot of Googlers

00:16:51.401 --> 00:16:53.240
are actually carrying
around iOS devices.

00:16:53.240 --> 00:16:54.948
And I'll explain what
their experience is

00:16:54.948 --> 00:16:56.510
like versus an Android device.

00:16:56.510 --> 00:16:59.240
You'll see a lot of Googlers
carrying Mac devices.

00:16:59.240 --> 00:17:00.370
Few are carrying Windows.

00:17:00.370 --> 00:17:01.890
But they have those as well.

00:17:01.890 --> 00:17:03.110
Lot of us carry Chrome.

00:17:03.110 --> 00:17:05.900
Actually, it's a completely
individual decision

00:17:05.900 --> 00:17:08.300
by every Googler which
device they want to get.

00:17:08.300 --> 00:17:09.440
You can just go to
the internal portal

00:17:09.440 --> 00:17:10.856
and say this is
the device I want.

00:17:10.856 --> 00:17:14.075
If you want when your
hardware refresh cycle is up,

00:17:14.075 --> 00:17:15.200
you can say, you know what?

00:17:15.200 --> 00:17:16.670
I've been on Mac OS.

00:17:16.670 --> 00:17:18.440
I want to go to
Chrome OS for a while.

00:17:18.440 --> 00:17:20.750
And you can absolutely do that.

00:17:20.750 --> 00:17:23.869
All of these platforms are
supported at the highest tier.

00:17:23.869 --> 00:17:29.044
We have the most amount of trust
in these particular platforms.

00:17:29.044 --> 00:17:30.460
From an application
standpoint, we

00:17:30.460 --> 00:17:32.080
use a variety of applications.

00:17:32.080 --> 00:17:34.960
Obviously, we are a G Suite
customer-- a very, very big G

00:17:34.960 --> 00:17:35.590
Suite customer.

00:17:35.590 --> 00:17:37.460
And we have all
those applications.

00:17:37.460 --> 00:17:40.250
And then we have-- and this
is kind of interesting.

00:17:40.250 --> 00:17:43.510
We do not have generically
named app applications.

00:17:43.510 --> 00:17:46.730
Like they probably do have
names on the other side.

00:17:46.730 --> 00:17:49.060
But we have the usual
third-party applications,

00:17:49.060 --> 00:17:50.760
which we also have access to.

00:17:50.760 --> 00:17:55.092
And we build across all of
those using our Google identity.

00:17:55.092 --> 00:17:56.800
So I don't know if
people are on G Suite.

00:17:56.800 --> 00:17:58.660
But if you're on G
Suite, you'll know

00:17:58.660 --> 00:18:00.981
that you can center your
entire identity on that Google

00:18:00.981 --> 00:18:01.480
identity.

00:18:01.480 --> 00:18:03.220
When you log into
various cloud services,

00:18:03.220 --> 00:18:05.440
you can say log in with
your Google identity.

00:18:05.440 --> 00:18:07.490
We completely
centralized around that.

00:18:07.490 --> 00:18:09.850
And that allows us
to taxonomize which

00:18:09.850 --> 00:18:14.810
applications we can get
access to at any given time.

00:18:14.810 --> 00:18:16.720
So how do we bring
these concepts together

00:18:16.720 --> 00:18:18.340
and sort of set the access?

00:18:18.340 --> 00:18:20.620
So I mentioned this before.

00:18:20.620 --> 00:18:23.410
And this is like a
really key point, right?

00:18:23.410 --> 00:18:27.340
Tiered access-- this
is one of those areas

00:18:27.340 --> 00:18:30.190
where having more
granular settings

00:18:30.190 --> 00:18:32.860
actually allows you to
have access much more

00:18:32.860 --> 00:18:36.950
flexibility in actually how you
grant access to certain things.

00:18:36.950 --> 00:18:39.820
So I've often had a
conversation with C-level folks

00:18:39.820 --> 00:18:42.010
from very big corporations
who just sort of say

00:18:42.010 --> 00:18:45.280
to me, Android, yes or no?

00:18:45.280 --> 00:18:46.960
Secure or not secure, right?

00:18:46.960 --> 00:18:49.210
Should I allow access
or not allow access?

00:18:49.210 --> 00:18:50.650
And we're like, hmm.

00:18:50.650 --> 00:18:53.170
Or they'll say, BYOD, yes or no?

00:18:53.170 --> 00:18:54.550
Secure or not secure?

00:18:54.550 --> 00:18:57.100
Should I allow access
or not allow access?

00:18:57.100 --> 00:19:00.040
And it's kind of
an awkward question

00:19:00.040 --> 00:19:02.890
to answer, right, because
it shouldn't be this binary,

00:19:02.890 --> 00:19:05.390
secure or not secure, right?

00:19:05.390 --> 00:19:08.830
One of the answers-- and
I'm the Android guy, right--

00:19:08.830 --> 00:19:11.632
is like, I said, listen,
I think BYOD is secure.

00:19:11.632 --> 00:19:13.340
And I can show you
how to make it secure.

00:19:13.340 --> 00:19:14.824
They're like, got
it, boom, secure.

00:19:14.824 --> 00:19:15.990
And I'm like, wait, hold on.

00:19:15.990 --> 00:19:18.700
Before you take
the secure box, I

00:19:18.700 --> 00:19:22.840
will say that I personally
would be extremely uncomfortable

00:19:22.840 --> 00:19:26.320
if your military generals are
carrying around nuclear codes

00:19:26.320 --> 00:19:28.450
on their BYOD phone, right?

00:19:28.450 --> 00:19:29.500
Really uncomfortable.

00:19:29.500 --> 00:19:31.450
I don't think you
should give out

00:19:31.450 --> 00:19:33.956
nuclear codes to BYOD devices.

00:19:33.956 --> 00:19:35.080
Then they're like, got it--

00:19:35.080 --> 00:19:35.980
insecure.

00:19:35.980 --> 00:19:38.320
And I'm like, OK, no.

00:19:38.320 --> 00:19:41.860
Like, please don't think
about it in this way, right?

00:19:41.860 --> 00:19:43.990
It's just not-- it
can't be these two boxes

00:19:43.990 --> 00:19:47.020
because it depends on what kind
of data you're carrying, right?

00:19:47.020 --> 00:19:49.210
So while most
companies hopefully

00:19:49.210 --> 00:19:53.950
do not have nuclear codes, there
are those crown jewel pieces

00:19:53.950 --> 00:19:55.810
of data that you
might want to have.

00:19:55.810 --> 00:19:59.620
So even though you
treat BYOD as secure,

00:19:59.620 --> 00:20:01.960
it would be not
unreasonable for you

00:20:01.960 --> 00:20:06.444
to give out dedicated corporate
tablets to your board.

00:20:06.444 --> 00:20:08.110
So that for boards
of directors meetings

00:20:08.110 --> 00:20:10.284
you can get the most
privileged information

00:20:10.284 --> 00:20:11.950
to the board of
directors without having

00:20:11.950 --> 00:20:15.324
to worry about and solve
a bunch of BYOD concerns.

00:20:15.324 --> 00:20:16.990
And that doesn't mean
you don't do BYOD.

00:20:16.990 --> 00:20:21.010
It just means you have to have
a nuanced, granular strategy

00:20:21.010 --> 00:20:22.370
to an approach.

00:20:22.370 --> 00:20:26.990
And so we do tiered access.

00:20:26.990 --> 00:20:28.690
The way that tiered
access works is

00:20:28.690 --> 00:20:31.330
that we classify the internal
services, like I said.

00:20:31.330 --> 00:20:33.940
That means that we declare
what's in the crown jewel

00:20:33.940 --> 00:20:35.857
category, the nuclear codes.

00:20:35.857 --> 00:20:36.940
We categorize [INAUDIBLE].

00:20:36.940 --> 00:20:37.774
Yeah, you know what?

00:20:37.774 --> 00:20:39.731
We probably need to know
that you're a Googler.

00:20:39.731 --> 00:20:41.290
And we need to know
that, in general,

00:20:41.290 --> 00:20:43.310
that you're in good standing
and all those kinds of things.

00:20:43.310 --> 00:20:44.800
And once we have that,
that's good enough.

00:20:44.800 --> 00:20:46.757
And we also [INAUDIBLE]
categorize, eh, we're

00:20:46.757 --> 00:20:47.590
just fine with this.

00:20:47.590 --> 00:20:49.406
Like, they can just
access that data,

00:20:49.406 --> 00:20:50.530
and it totally makes sense.

00:20:53.230 --> 00:20:55.420
Also, in order for
this to work, we

00:20:55.420 --> 00:20:57.910
have to know what state
your devices are in.

00:20:57.910 --> 00:20:59.850
We have to know to
help us do security.

00:20:59.850 --> 00:21:01.497
And we also-- and
this is implied.

00:21:01.497 --> 00:21:03.580
I'm not going to talk about
it specifically here--

00:21:03.580 --> 00:21:06.250
need to centralize access.

00:21:06.250 --> 00:21:10.572
So while we don't have the
VPN, we do have something--

00:21:10.572 --> 00:21:12.030
and [INAUDIBLE],
once again, talked

00:21:12.030 --> 00:21:13.580
about exactly what we do here.

00:21:13.580 --> 00:21:17.710
We do centralize all the
access to our services.

00:21:17.710 --> 00:21:20.860
So I'm going to just
skip over this for now

00:21:20.860 --> 00:21:23.290
and talk about the
centralization.

00:21:23.290 --> 00:21:26.690
In the centralization, we
have access control engines

00:21:26.690 --> 00:21:29.540
and trust inferrers that
sit between the endpoints

00:21:29.540 --> 00:21:31.300
and access to all
of our applications.

00:21:31.300 --> 00:21:33.200
Now, remember, we
are cloud native.

00:21:33.200 --> 00:21:36.290
We completely admitted this is
one of our biggest strengths

00:21:36.290 --> 00:21:38.337
is that since everything
sits in the cloud,

00:21:38.337 --> 00:21:39.920
there's a limited
number of protocols.

00:21:39.920 --> 00:21:43.350
It's like almost everything
flows over HTTP and HTPS,

00:21:43.350 --> 00:21:44.180
right?

00:21:44.180 --> 00:21:46.170
So we're able to limit
access to our services

00:21:46.170 --> 00:21:47.795
by having a limited
number of protocols

00:21:47.795 --> 00:21:49.355
and a limited number
of ports and all

00:21:49.355 --> 00:21:50.370
this kind of good stuff.

00:21:50.370 --> 00:21:53.060
So while we don't have a
traditional internet-exernet

00:21:53.060 --> 00:21:55.640
service, what we've done
is move that concept

00:21:55.640 --> 00:21:57.780
into the application layer.

00:21:57.780 --> 00:22:00.830
So our actual HTTP and our
proxies and our actual servers

00:22:00.830 --> 00:22:04.220
are able to be aware of
the state of a device

00:22:04.220 --> 00:22:08.084
before granting that access
over to a particular service

00:22:08.084 --> 00:22:08.750
on the back end.

00:22:08.750 --> 00:22:10.850
And the services are
themselves also aware

00:22:10.850 --> 00:22:12.500
of the endpoint accessing them.

00:22:12.500 --> 00:22:16.070
So we're not saying we have to
issue you a private IP in order

00:22:16.070 --> 00:22:17.550
for this to actually work.

00:22:17.550 --> 00:22:20.270
It's not a
network-level concept.

00:22:20.270 --> 00:22:23.720
And the same exact proxies
are used to access services,

00:22:23.720 --> 00:22:25.700
no matter which network I'm on.

00:22:25.700 --> 00:22:28.520
The only thing that changes
is the level of trust changes.

00:22:28.520 --> 00:22:30.730
Because we're able to analyze
when I'm on a Starbucks

00:22:30.730 --> 00:22:33.200
network, yes, that is a
different security posture

00:22:33.200 --> 00:22:35.000
than when I'm on my
home network, which

00:22:35.000 --> 00:22:37.830
is a different security posture
than when I'm on my intranet.

00:22:37.830 --> 00:22:39.300
That is definitely true.

00:22:39.300 --> 00:22:42.170
But we don't use on a
on-net/off-net model to try

00:22:42.170 --> 00:22:44.970
and define the access
model from that point.

00:22:44.970 --> 00:22:46.220
So we do have this.

00:22:46.220 --> 00:22:48.950
If you look at-- we've actually
launched a publicly available

00:22:48.950 --> 00:22:53.120
version of this which is called
our Gateway product, which

00:22:53.120 --> 00:22:54.380
was launching here at Next.

00:22:54.380 --> 00:22:56.570
It's coming into beta
and a trusted tester.

00:22:56.570 --> 00:22:58.880
And it's something where we
make this proxy technology

00:22:58.880 --> 00:23:01.490
available to other customers.

00:23:01.490 --> 00:23:03.980
Combined with the
actual proxy here,

00:23:03.980 --> 00:23:06.440
the Gatekeeper,
what we actually use

00:23:06.440 --> 00:23:09.500
is a trust engine, an
access control engine

00:23:09.500 --> 00:23:11.625
which is saying, all
right, so I have everything

00:23:11.625 --> 00:23:12.500
funneled through now.

00:23:12.500 --> 00:23:13.875
It's all gone
through my proxies.

00:23:13.875 --> 00:23:15.260
It's protecting my services.

00:23:15.260 --> 00:23:17.420
I know how secure my service is.

00:23:17.420 --> 00:23:20.870
So what do I want to do in
terms of actually granting

00:23:20.870 --> 00:23:23.000
access or not granting access?

00:23:23.000 --> 00:23:25.760
And in this case, here are a
few examples of what we do.

00:23:25.760 --> 00:23:28.020
We actually are always
collecting data,

00:23:28.020 --> 00:23:32.334
and we collect this data
using our own internal EMM.

00:23:32.334 --> 00:23:33.750
So we actually use
the use G Suite

00:23:33.750 --> 00:23:35.520
EM to do it, plus some
tools that we developed

00:23:35.520 --> 00:23:36.620
and scripts we developed.

00:23:36.620 --> 00:23:39.800
So this is collecting
logs, endpoint status,

00:23:39.800 --> 00:23:41.740
reporting on what's
going on the endpoint,

00:23:41.740 --> 00:23:43.740
reporting what applications
are on the endpoint,

00:23:43.740 --> 00:23:46.220
seeing whether we think there's
any known malware there--

00:23:46.220 --> 00:23:47.860
lots and lots and
lots of data, which

00:23:47.860 --> 00:23:50.510
is being collected from our
endpoints at all times--

00:23:50.510 --> 00:23:53.930
and actually saying, OK,
what is the level of trust

00:23:53.930 --> 00:23:55.800
we put on this
particular device?

00:23:55.800 --> 00:23:58.130
And therefore, in
real time, we can

00:23:58.130 --> 00:24:01.230
adjust the security posturing
of those particular devices.

00:24:01.230 --> 00:24:04.040
So if today we suddenly find
out, hey, you know what?

00:24:04.040 --> 00:24:05.930
There's a newly discovered
class of malware

00:24:05.930 --> 00:24:08.534
running on our Windows
laptops, for example.

00:24:08.534 --> 00:24:09.950
We didn't know
about it yesterday.

00:24:09.950 --> 00:24:11.120
We know about it today.

00:24:11.120 --> 00:24:13.760
We can actually-- instead
of saying, well, we

00:24:13.760 --> 00:24:15.569
will go on and say,
let's go and scan

00:24:15.569 --> 00:24:17.360
and let's flash and
remove malware and make

00:24:17.360 --> 00:24:18.590
sure we do that in the fleet.

00:24:18.590 --> 00:24:20.870
But to make sure we have
immediate protection,

00:24:20.870 --> 00:24:23.035
we can say, let's change
the trust engine to say

00:24:23.035 --> 00:24:25.160
that if we know that this
particular application is

00:24:25.160 --> 00:24:28.490
installed on a Windows
machine, no longer allow access

00:24:28.490 --> 00:24:31.290
from that Windows machines to
are most secure technology,

00:24:31.290 --> 00:24:31.790
right?

00:24:31.790 --> 00:24:34.310
So therefore, because
that's actually in our cloud

00:24:34.310 --> 00:24:36.680
and on the endpoint who's
determining the access,

00:24:36.680 --> 00:24:38.300
we are able to move them down.

00:24:38.300 --> 00:24:40.760
And that that user can
probably still get email.

00:24:40.760 --> 00:24:42.320
They can still do
their regular work.

00:24:42.320 --> 00:24:44.270
But they might not be
able to access the crown

00:24:44.270 --> 00:24:46.675
jewels at that point
until they're remediated.

00:24:46.675 --> 00:24:48.050
And then once
they're remediated,

00:24:48.050 --> 00:24:50.180
they can get access
again to everything else.

00:24:50.180 --> 00:24:51.864
So we collect all of this data.

00:24:51.864 --> 00:24:53.030
And we're able to crunch it.

00:24:53.030 --> 00:24:54.060
We use machine learning.

00:24:54.060 --> 00:24:55.940
We use our-- we have a
security analyst team.

00:24:55.940 --> 00:24:56.990
We have a lot of
different teams.

00:24:56.990 --> 00:24:59.030
You can imagine what we're
doing there to make sure

00:24:59.030 --> 00:25:00.155
that everything is working.

00:25:03.640 --> 00:25:06.970
So tiers of access--
let's sort of get down

00:25:06.970 --> 00:25:10.510
to some brass tacks.

00:25:10.510 --> 00:25:12.890
There's actually another
tier here called untrusted.

00:25:12.890 --> 00:25:14.140
But we're getting rid of that.

00:25:14.140 --> 00:25:15.850
So there's minimally
trusted, mostly trusted,

00:25:15.850 --> 00:25:17.020
and fully trusted, right?

00:25:17.020 --> 00:25:19.162
Very easy language to use.

00:25:19.162 --> 00:25:19.870
You can map this.

00:25:19.870 --> 00:25:21.578
You can take it, use
at your own company.

00:25:21.578 --> 00:25:22.960
You can map it to something.

00:25:22.960 --> 00:25:26.290
Minimally trusted
is basic access.

00:25:26.290 --> 00:25:28.600
And what we mean by that
is stuff where you're like,

00:25:28.600 --> 00:25:31.230
OK, you need to do your job.

00:25:31.230 --> 00:25:34.180
And to be really honest, if
someone who's not a Googler

00:25:34.180 --> 00:25:38.190
looked at this stuff, it's not
really the end of the world,

00:25:38.190 --> 00:25:38.690
right?

00:25:40.860 --> 00:25:43.360
But it's not just
the most basic stuff.

00:25:43.360 --> 00:25:46.300
But it's just stuff where don't
really try and care too much.

00:25:46.300 --> 00:25:48.370
Definitely stuff like
our bus schedules

00:25:48.370 --> 00:25:49.660
would be in there, right?

00:25:49.660 --> 00:25:53.554
Our menu and cafeteria
access in there.

00:25:53.554 --> 00:25:54.220
That's in there.

00:25:54.220 --> 00:25:56.260
But interestingly,
and a lot of customers

00:25:56.260 --> 00:25:58.570
find this interesting,
we actually

00:25:58.570 --> 00:26:01.520
put email in that
category as well,

00:26:01.520 --> 00:26:05.359
because we find email to
be extensional for someone

00:26:05.359 --> 00:26:06.650
to actually get their job done.

00:26:06.650 --> 00:26:08.690
And so we want them to be
able to get their job done

00:26:08.690 --> 00:26:09.750
in a lot of different places.

00:26:09.750 --> 00:26:11.625
So emails, doc sheets
and slides are actually

00:26:11.625 --> 00:26:14.140
all in the minimally
trusted category.

00:26:14.140 --> 00:26:17.500
So if you bring in
BYOD today to Google,

00:26:17.500 --> 00:26:19.840
you can bring in
any BYOD to phone.

00:26:19.840 --> 00:26:22.170
And you can get your email
on that phone, right?

00:26:22.170 --> 00:26:24.520
I know a lot of you probably
have like an approach where

00:26:24.520 --> 00:26:25.480
you can't do that.

00:26:25.480 --> 00:26:26.800
You can do that.

00:26:26.800 --> 00:26:27.580
We trust that.

00:26:27.580 --> 00:26:29.350
We trust the Gmail
suite of applications.

00:26:29.350 --> 00:26:32.710
We trust G Suite and
the inherent security

00:26:32.710 --> 00:26:37.170
built into the cloud to
keep all of that stuff safe.

00:26:37.170 --> 00:26:40.710
Mostly trusted is normal access.

00:26:40.710 --> 00:26:43.050
And what we mean by
that is, OK, we're

00:26:43.050 --> 00:26:47.280
going to have a stronger
position around exactly

00:26:47.280 --> 00:26:48.570
what your identity is.

00:26:48.570 --> 00:26:52.920
We can have a better idea about
the state your device is in.

00:26:52.920 --> 00:26:55.590
You should get access
to most applications

00:26:55.590 --> 00:26:59.760
to get your job done if you're
sitting in mostly trusted.

00:26:59.760 --> 00:27:02.921
You shouldn't have to go to
all the way to fully trusted

00:27:02.921 --> 00:27:04.170
in order to get your job done.

00:27:04.170 --> 00:27:05.790
Because I know people are
probably already thinking,

00:27:05.790 --> 00:27:07.770
oh, OK, well, that must
mean that fully trusted

00:27:07.770 --> 00:27:08.751
is the best tier.

00:27:08.751 --> 00:27:10.500
We must put everything
into fully trusted,

00:27:10.500 --> 00:27:11.681
and then just, oh, I'm done.

00:27:11.681 --> 00:27:13.680
But if you do that, once
again, you don't really

00:27:13.680 --> 00:27:15.200
have a tiered strategy, right?

00:27:15.200 --> 00:27:16.950
You, once again, have
the binary strategy,

00:27:16.950 --> 00:27:18.750
where you're either full
or you're not fully.

00:27:18.750 --> 00:27:20.249
And if you're not
fully, then you're

00:27:20.249 --> 00:27:22.330
just a straight-up
second-class citizen.

00:27:22.330 --> 00:27:23.350
That's not our approach.

00:27:23.350 --> 00:27:25.410
Our approach is if
we're doing a good job,

00:27:25.410 --> 00:27:28.020
you should be able to do the
vast majority of your job day

00:27:28.020 --> 00:27:31.190
to day, sitting in a
mostly trusted tier.

00:27:31.190 --> 00:27:33.930
And you should be able to
most of your job sitting

00:27:33.930 --> 00:27:35.800
in a minimally trusted--

00:27:35.800 --> 00:27:38.400
a good amount of your job in
the minimally trusted tier.

00:27:38.400 --> 00:27:43.950
But certain things you might
have to go to fully trusted on.

00:27:43.950 --> 00:27:47.280
So as an example,
when I go home,

00:27:47.280 --> 00:27:49.410
I need to have a
two-factor authentication

00:27:49.410 --> 00:27:51.060
to get into mostly
trusted, right?

00:27:51.060 --> 00:27:53.520
But I can take my
two-factor authentication,

00:27:53.520 --> 00:27:56.280
and I can use it with
my home computer.

00:27:56.280 --> 00:27:57.582
That's my computer that I own.

00:27:57.582 --> 00:27:58.540
And so it's not imaged.

00:27:58.540 --> 00:27:59.440
It's not flashed.

00:27:59.440 --> 00:28:00.750
It's not anything like that.

00:28:00.750 --> 00:28:03.030
It also doesn't give up
a lot of logs or anything

00:28:03.030 --> 00:28:03.766
back to Google.

00:28:03.766 --> 00:28:05.140
I can put my
two-factor token in.

00:28:05.140 --> 00:28:08.190
I can get into that middle
mostly trusted state,

00:28:08.190 --> 00:28:11.400
and I can do the vast
majority of my job

00:28:11.400 --> 00:28:15.230
on that particular computer
when I work from home.

00:28:15.230 --> 00:28:18.930
[INAUDIBLE], again,
very simple examples--

00:28:18.930 --> 00:28:22.920
cafe menu, probably totally
fine to be at that lower tier.

00:28:22.920 --> 00:28:25.450
There's not a lot you're
going to do with that.

00:28:25.450 --> 00:28:28.230
But I'm not going to be
able to go and check even

00:28:28.230 --> 00:28:32.070
my own payroll status from
that particular device.

00:28:32.070 --> 00:28:34.170
And I'm not going to
be able to go and check

00:28:34.170 --> 00:28:37.350
in code, download
code, certainly not

00:28:37.350 --> 00:28:40.320
push the production machines.

00:28:40.320 --> 00:28:44.094
Again, that middle tier, where
I'm able to do most of my job--

00:28:44.094 --> 00:28:45.510
and remember, I'm
not an engineer.

00:28:45.510 --> 00:28:48.400
So when I say most of my job, I
don't need to code for a living

00:28:48.400 --> 00:28:49.650
because this isn't my product.

00:28:49.650 --> 00:28:51.225
But tighter controls there.

00:28:51.225 --> 00:28:53.350
And we have applications
that sit in that category,

00:28:53.350 --> 00:28:55.800
so now I can access more
private, more secure

00:28:55.800 --> 00:28:58.440
information, where really
being me is important.

00:28:58.440 --> 00:29:00.300
Remember, now it
requires two-factor

00:29:00.300 --> 00:29:01.380
in order to get in there.

00:29:01.380 --> 00:29:04.230
So OK, wonderful, shorter
lived access tokens,

00:29:04.230 --> 00:29:07.440
shorter lived identity
tokens, requires two factors

00:29:07.440 --> 00:29:08.220
to get in there.

00:29:08.220 --> 00:29:09.840
But now if you do
all those things,

00:29:09.840 --> 00:29:12.510
you're able to go in and
check payroll and do--

00:29:12.510 --> 00:29:15.780
I can approve expense
reports and those kind

00:29:15.780 --> 00:29:18.400
of things in that category.

00:29:18.400 --> 00:29:21.900
And finally-- the last tier,
again, based on your business,

00:29:21.900 --> 00:29:23.490
all these things will apply.

00:29:23.490 --> 00:29:25.410
For us, our crown
jewels, we're Google,

00:29:25.410 --> 00:29:27.660
our crown jewels is
our code and our cloud

00:29:27.660 --> 00:29:29.010
and our production base, right?

00:29:29.010 --> 00:29:32.820
So we do require you to be
on a fully trusted, fully

00:29:32.820 --> 00:29:34.560
interrogated machine
in order to be

00:29:34.560 --> 00:29:38.330
able to access that data to do
those things here at Google.

00:29:43.485 --> 00:29:45.360
So how are we actually
doing this on laptops?

00:29:45.360 --> 00:29:46.600
I referred to
mobile a little bit.

00:29:46.600 --> 00:29:48.433
This is kind of to the
world a lot of people

00:29:48.433 --> 00:29:50.470
are sitting in right now.

00:29:50.470 --> 00:29:52.510
And we do this as well, right?

00:29:52.510 --> 00:29:55.270
Like, we do this for older OSes.

00:29:55.270 --> 00:29:56.200
We have the virus.

00:29:56.200 --> 00:29:57.160
We have antivirus.

00:29:57.160 --> 00:29:59.050
We have our own
images from Mac OS.

00:29:59.050 --> 00:30:01.517
We have our own
images for Windows.

00:30:01.517 --> 00:30:03.100
We're also constantly
upgrading those.

00:30:03.100 --> 00:30:05.770
We're going to Patch
Tuesday stuff for Windows.

00:30:05.770 --> 00:30:06.900
We issue the patches.

00:30:06.900 --> 00:30:09.010
We do everything that your
companies are probably

00:30:09.010 --> 00:30:11.450
doing to make sure that
that fleet stays up to date.

00:30:11.450 --> 00:30:12.940
If you're not
doing those things,

00:30:12.940 --> 00:30:15.370
you should probably be
doing those things on any

00:30:15.370 --> 00:30:16.750
of your operating systems.

00:30:16.750 --> 00:30:18.600
So we do go through this but.

00:30:18.600 --> 00:30:19.934
We don't love this world, right?

00:30:19.934 --> 00:30:22.308
We don't think this is the
modern way for people to live.

00:30:22.308 --> 00:30:23.517
Again, it feels constraining.

00:30:23.517 --> 00:30:24.933
It feels like busy
work that needs

00:30:24.933 --> 00:30:26.710
to be done just for
the sake of security,

00:30:26.710 --> 00:30:28.540
not really enabling
anything good.

00:30:28.540 --> 00:30:31.690
So what we did is for
both Chrome and Android--

00:30:31.690 --> 00:30:34.730
and here I will give the
example of using Chrome OS.

00:30:34.730 --> 00:30:41.025
Chrome OS is actually fully
trusted out of the box.

00:30:41.025 --> 00:30:43.150
Windows is not fully trusted
out of the box, right?

00:30:43.150 --> 00:30:46.926
I cannot go and get it
off-the-shelf Windows laptop,

00:30:46.926 --> 00:30:48.550
bring it in, and say,
yeah, yeah, yeah,

00:30:48.550 --> 00:30:51.050
this is a Google laptop, and
then bring it to fully trusted.

00:30:51.050 --> 00:30:52.840
It has to run our Windows image.

00:30:52.840 --> 00:30:54.670
Mac OS has to run our Mac image.

00:30:54.670 --> 00:30:58.930
Chrome OS does not run
a special Google image.

00:30:58.930 --> 00:31:01.175
It uses the consumer
image, right?

00:31:01.175 --> 00:31:03.300
This is why I'm giving this
example out of the box.

00:31:03.300 --> 00:31:04.570
Same thing for Android--

00:31:04.570 --> 00:31:07.030
do not have its own
image for Android,

00:31:07.030 --> 00:31:11.360
runs the consumer image, yet
can get into fully trusted--

00:31:11.360 --> 00:31:14.300
can get into that highest tier
of security for us, right?

00:31:14.300 --> 00:31:16.540
Again, I'll just
reiterate to set

00:31:16.540 --> 00:31:19.321
your intuition, Windows laptop
at home, using it at home,

00:31:19.321 --> 00:31:19.820
great.

00:31:19.820 --> 00:31:23.260
You can get into minimally for
sure, maybe even into mostly.

00:31:23.260 --> 00:31:25.720
But to get into
fully, you're going

00:31:25.720 --> 00:31:28.360
to always have to have an
image running from Google

00:31:28.360 --> 00:31:29.990
in this particular case.

00:31:29.990 --> 00:31:32.130
Chrome, that's not the case.

00:31:32.130 --> 00:31:34.660
And that's because Chrome
and Android and iOS,

00:31:34.660 --> 00:31:36.580
right, are running
in a more modern way

00:31:36.580 --> 00:31:38.110
of doing operating systems.

00:31:38.110 --> 00:31:41.170
And Chrome, in this case, has
verified boot TPMs, encryption

00:31:41.170 --> 00:31:44.454
out of the box, sandboxing of
the actual processes, and all

00:31:44.454 --> 00:31:45.120
of these things.

00:31:45.120 --> 00:31:47.650
So verify boot, for
example, we feel

00:31:47.650 --> 00:31:51.430
comfortable because we know
that the Chrome image is always

00:31:51.430 --> 00:31:52.060
signed.

00:31:52.060 --> 00:31:53.500
It has tamper protection.

00:31:53.500 --> 00:31:55.090
It has tamper resistance.

00:31:55.090 --> 00:31:58.300
And so we know it is always
using a Google signed

00:31:58.300 --> 00:32:01.540
from the manufacturer
firmware image

00:32:01.540 --> 00:32:02.690
on that particular device.

00:32:02.690 --> 00:32:04.773
And we have the same thing
on Android now as well.

00:32:04.773 --> 00:32:06.790
So you might don't know
this, but on Android,

00:32:06.790 --> 00:32:08.980
if someone wants to flash
a custom ROM, et cetera,

00:32:08.980 --> 00:32:11.140
you can absolutely detect
that through your EMM,

00:32:11.140 --> 00:32:13.230
and either prevent it,
or you can say, well,

00:32:13.230 --> 00:32:16.750
if someone has a custom ROM,
no fully trusted access, which

00:32:16.750 --> 00:32:18.970
is what we do at Google, right?

00:32:18.970 --> 00:32:20.660
So you have to be
using the manufacturer

00:32:20.660 --> 00:32:23.020
ROM and the manufacturer OS.

00:32:23.020 --> 00:32:27.170
We leveraged a TPM on Chrome
OS for storing secure keys.

00:32:27.170 --> 00:32:29.840
So if we're going to give a
long-lasting identity token,

00:32:29.840 --> 00:32:31.631
so this is something
we try and do in fully

00:32:31.631 --> 00:32:33.550
trusted is you don't
want to be logging in

00:32:33.550 --> 00:32:36.920
over and over and over and over
again for security purposes.

00:32:36.920 --> 00:32:39.205
So we need a way to give
long-lasting identity

00:32:39.205 --> 00:32:42.310
and long-lasting OAuth tokens
and identity tokens out

00:32:42.310 --> 00:32:43.760
to the particular devices.

00:32:43.760 --> 00:32:46.270
So by using the TPM
as the route of trust,

00:32:46.270 --> 00:32:49.120
we're able to actually do
that with higher confidence,

00:32:49.120 --> 00:32:52.240
rather than having really
important tokens sitting

00:32:52.240 --> 00:32:55.630
in somewhere on disk where
that can be extracted and used

00:32:55.630 --> 00:32:56.676
by an attacker, right?

00:32:56.676 --> 00:32:58.300
So that's an important
thing is to have

00:32:58.300 --> 00:32:59.910
a appropriate route of trust.

00:32:59.910 --> 00:33:06.946
Processor sandboxing-- processes
sandboxing-- so inherently,

00:33:06.946 --> 00:33:08.320
everything's run
its own process.

00:33:08.320 --> 00:33:10.100
Those processes are
protected against each other.

00:33:10.100 --> 00:33:11.350
So if you have a
piece of malware,

00:33:11.350 --> 00:33:13.630
it's very difficult for it
to attack another process.

00:33:13.630 --> 00:33:16.610
It's difficult for it to extract
data from another directory.

00:33:16.610 --> 00:33:19.900
All of this is built in, again,
from the ground up by default

00:33:19.900 --> 00:33:22.254
in modern operating systems.

00:33:22.254 --> 00:33:24.670
There's a point there that
some of you may not understand,

00:33:24.670 --> 00:33:28.700
which is we actually also have
the Play Store and Android

00:33:28.700 --> 00:33:30.724
applications now
running on Chrome OS.

00:33:30.724 --> 00:33:32.890
And that is also running
in a separate process which

00:33:32.890 --> 00:33:35.449
is isolated from the main
kernel so that we can actually

00:33:35.449 --> 00:33:37.240
make sure that we have
high enough security

00:33:37.240 --> 00:33:38.845
from any Android app
that can be downloaded.

00:33:38.845 --> 00:33:40.386
It does not interfere
with any of any

00:33:40.386 --> 00:33:43.180
of the security on
the Chrome OS side.

00:33:43.180 --> 00:33:44.890
And of course updates, right?

00:33:44.890 --> 00:33:47.080
And this is a really
painful thing.

00:33:47.080 --> 00:33:48.640
A lot of investment
going into here.

00:33:48.640 --> 00:33:53.140
But on Chrome OS,
0% IT effort is

00:33:53.140 --> 00:33:56.341
spent into managing
updates, managing patches,

00:33:56.341 --> 00:33:58.840
like figuring out what's going
on or doing all those things.

00:33:58.840 --> 00:34:02.650
We use the exact same
over-the-air, every-six-week

00:34:02.650 --> 00:34:07.960
automatic update process that
a regular Chrome OS consumer

00:34:07.960 --> 00:34:09.067
user would use, right?

00:34:09.067 --> 00:34:10.900
Because everything is
baked into that cycle.

00:34:10.900 --> 00:34:12.405
We can-- we don't here.

00:34:12.405 --> 00:34:14.530
But just speaking for how
you can manage Chrome OS,

00:34:14.530 --> 00:34:16.360
it has a full set
of EMM controls

00:34:16.360 --> 00:34:17.770
that you can use with it.

00:34:17.770 --> 00:34:20.590
You can delay those updates
because sometimes you

00:34:20.590 --> 00:34:22.840
want to do verification
checking and testing and stuff

00:34:22.840 --> 00:34:23.420
like that.

00:34:23.420 --> 00:34:25.480
But because of the nature of
Chrome OS, because everything

00:34:25.480 --> 00:34:27.100
is really running
through the browser,

00:34:27.100 --> 00:34:30.080
because we're, once
again, cloud native,

00:34:30.080 --> 00:34:32.230
our need to actually
go and say, oh,

00:34:32.230 --> 00:34:34.510
let me test all my
applications is almost 0

00:34:34.510 --> 00:34:36.969
because we don't have that
legacy application burden

00:34:36.969 --> 00:34:39.750
that we do on other platforms.

00:34:39.750 --> 00:34:42.860
So the benefits of this
approach is that it's

00:34:42.860 --> 00:34:45.380
greatly simplified for IT.

00:34:45.380 --> 00:34:47.300
We know that we're
secure by default,

00:34:47.300 --> 00:34:50.449
so we're able to grant that
high tier of access to Chrome OS

00:34:50.449 --> 00:34:54.020
and to Android just
out of the box, right?

00:34:54.020 --> 00:34:56.630
And it has a wonderful
employee experience

00:34:56.630 --> 00:34:58.602
because you can just get
up and running and not

00:34:58.602 --> 00:34:59.810
have to worry about anything.

00:34:59.810 --> 00:35:02.420
So I'll give you an example
of what I did yesterday.

00:35:02.420 --> 00:35:03.795
You might have
noticed that we're

00:35:03.795 --> 00:35:06.800
presenting from a lot of
Chrome OS devices here at Next.

00:35:06.800 --> 00:35:08.510
But what happened
was, is I needed

00:35:08.510 --> 00:35:13.460
to present from my laptop,
not this presentation,

00:35:13.460 --> 00:35:15.480
but at a different
one somewhere else.

00:35:15.480 --> 00:35:17.730
And so what actually happened
was I was like, well,

00:35:17.730 --> 00:35:19.220
I have it all set
up on my laptop

00:35:19.220 --> 00:35:21.261
and I could get
access to it up here.

00:35:21.261 --> 00:35:23.510
And it's a privileged document
that I want to present,

00:35:23.510 --> 00:35:26.220
a privileged site
that I need to get to.

00:35:26.220 --> 00:35:28.580
So instead of saying, well,
how do I unplug this laptop

00:35:28.580 --> 00:35:31.080
and plug everything in and get
it all working on the screen,

00:35:31.080 --> 00:35:34.460
everything like that, all I had
to do was go to that device,

00:35:34.460 --> 00:35:36.100
and I just said, log
in as a new user.

00:35:36.100 --> 00:35:38.870
I've never logged into this
device before-- never, right?

00:35:38.870 --> 00:35:40.370
I just said log
in as a new user.

00:35:40.370 --> 00:35:41.760
Log in as Andrew.

00:35:41.760 --> 00:35:43.790
It's already a privilege device.

00:35:43.790 --> 00:35:45.470
I put in my security key.

00:35:45.470 --> 00:35:46.220
I logged in.

00:35:46.220 --> 00:35:49.880
I got all access to
everything immediately

00:35:49.880 --> 00:35:51.372
from my own drive
and my own docs

00:35:51.372 --> 00:35:53.330
and everything, pulled
up at the site I needed.

00:35:53.330 --> 00:35:54.470
Pull up the site-- great.

00:35:54.470 --> 00:35:55.510
Did my presentation.

00:35:55.510 --> 00:35:57.330
Then logged out, said
destroy the session,

00:35:57.330 --> 00:36:01.070
and everything is removed
from the Chrome device, right?

00:36:01.070 --> 00:36:05.210
Completely secure-- but I
can use any Chrome device

00:36:05.210 --> 00:36:08.330
in the Google fleet and--

00:36:08.330 --> 00:36:09.980
I'm just saying--
within a minute,

00:36:09.980 --> 00:36:11.662
I had that absolutely
set up to be me,

00:36:11.662 --> 00:36:13.745
just the way I want it,
with all of my access, all

00:36:13.745 --> 00:36:15.889
of my privileges,
everything I need.

00:36:15.889 --> 00:36:18.180
So that's what we mean by
enhanced employee experience.

00:36:18.180 --> 00:36:20.138
And again, we don't force
people to use Chrome.

00:36:20.138 --> 00:36:21.750
It's a choice that
people can make.

00:36:21.750 --> 00:36:22.350
They can get a Mac.

00:36:22.350 --> 00:36:23.600
They can get a Windows device.

00:36:23.600 --> 00:36:26.410
But there's some nice
advantages of using Chrome.

00:36:26.410 --> 00:36:30.160
So this is another
example, Chrome Grab'n Go.

00:36:30.160 --> 00:36:33.850
This is what I just said
but in the form of sometimes

00:36:33.850 --> 00:36:36.460
I leave my laptop at home
because I'm absent minded.

00:36:36.460 --> 00:36:38.140
And this happens
actually quite a lot.

00:36:38.140 --> 00:36:41.230
So as long as I carry my
security key, what I can do

00:36:41.230 --> 00:36:43.750
is we just have these massive
banks of Chromebooks that

00:36:43.750 --> 00:36:45.544
just sit in every building.

00:36:45.544 --> 00:36:46.960
And if you forget
your laptop, you

00:36:46.960 --> 00:36:50.627
can just go, grab a generic
one, no IT interference.

00:36:50.627 --> 00:36:51.710
You don't even talk to IT.

00:36:51.710 --> 00:36:53.110
You don't even file a ticket.

00:36:53.110 --> 00:36:53.830
You just grab it.

00:36:53.830 --> 00:36:55.413
They're all getting
power right there.

00:36:55.413 --> 00:36:56.794
You stick in your security key.

00:36:56.794 --> 00:36:58.960
You set it up for yourself
just the way I just said.

00:36:58.960 --> 00:37:00.989
You just log in basically
with your credential.

00:37:00.989 --> 00:37:01.780
You use it all day.

00:37:01.780 --> 00:37:03.580
And because I'm
a Chrome OS user,

00:37:03.580 --> 00:37:07.570
it feels exactly the same as
using my own laptop, right?

00:37:07.570 --> 00:37:09.340
I use it for the day.

00:37:09.340 --> 00:37:11.620
When I'm done with it, I
just stick it back in there,

00:37:11.620 --> 00:37:13.272
and I just put
the power back in.

00:37:13.272 --> 00:37:14.230
And it says, OK, great.

00:37:14.230 --> 00:37:16.630
Thanks for returning your
Chromebook, and I go home.

00:37:16.630 --> 00:37:17.770
That's it, right?

00:37:17.770 --> 00:37:19.510
So it's completely self-service.

00:37:19.510 --> 00:37:20.920
It's completely secure.

00:37:20.920 --> 00:37:22.090
We didn't make it secure.

00:37:22.090 --> 00:37:24.250
It's literally just the
same as my own laptop.

00:37:24.250 --> 00:37:25.750
The only reason I
have my own laptop

00:37:25.750 --> 00:37:29.320
is because I just like the
idea of ownership, I guess.

00:37:29.320 --> 00:37:32.786
But really, from actually a
getting-my-job-done standpoint,

00:37:32.786 --> 00:37:35.410
there's no difference, literally
no difference between me using

00:37:35.410 --> 00:37:37.630
my own laptop and using
one of these loaners,

00:37:37.630 --> 00:37:40.460
other than the fact, I like the
idea that I have my own laptop.

00:37:43.310 --> 00:37:45.840
So how do you take this-- we're
going to have plenty of room

00:37:45.840 --> 00:37:46.881
for questions at the end.

00:37:46.881 --> 00:37:50.020
I can take this
anywhere you want.

00:37:50.020 --> 00:37:52.360
So how do we actually
plug this in?

00:37:52.360 --> 00:37:54.400
Building a phased
approach-- so you definitely

00:37:54.400 --> 00:37:57.302
want to start slowly
and start looking.

00:37:57.302 --> 00:37:59.260
You don't want to just
revolutionize and change

00:37:59.260 --> 00:38:01.210
your entire thing and say, we're
going to BeyondCorp at once.

00:38:01.210 --> 00:38:03.130
But you want to say, how
do you build the best

00:38:03.130 --> 00:38:04.510
experience for your users?

00:38:04.510 --> 00:38:07.930
How do you make sure that
you've identified the users who

00:38:07.930 --> 00:38:10.330
need access to things, gone
through your applications,

00:38:10.330 --> 00:38:12.010
taxonomized your
applications, made

00:38:12.010 --> 00:38:14.020
sure you know what
people are accessing,

00:38:14.020 --> 00:38:17.620
and get all that
stuff under control.

00:38:17.620 --> 00:38:20.652
You want to have a dedicated
team working with your devices.

00:38:20.652 --> 00:38:22.360
I'm just guessing most
of you probably do

00:38:22.360 --> 00:38:23.350
because you're in this session.

00:38:23.350 --> 00:38:24.700
You're probably
part of this team.

00:38:24.700 --> 00:38:26.200
But you do want
happy people who are

00:38:26.200 --> 00:38:27.550
thinking about this every day.

00:38:27.550 --> 00:38:30.040
Because if they're
not, once again, you're

00:38:30.040 --> 00:38:31.570
not going to have a strategy.

00:38:31.570 --> 00:38:33.364
You're just going to
have tactics, right?

00:38:33.364 --> 00:38:35.530
So you want to have a
strategy for your organization

00:38:35.530 --> 00:38:36.504
that's right for you.

00:38:36.504 --> 00:38:38.920
We think that the concepts
I've talked about today-- again

00:38:38.920 --> 00:38:39.850
they're high level.

00:38:39.850 --> 00:38:41.647
But they apply to
95% of companies.

00:38:41.647 --> 00:38:43.480
But that doesn't mean
you can just take them

00:38:43.480 --> 00:38:44.560
and immediately do them.

00:38:44.560 --> 00:38:47.080
You do need to make sure you
do the work to adapt them

00:38:47.080 --> 00:38:48.850
to your company's needs.

00:38:48.850 --> 00:38:51.250
So you need a team
who's working on that.

00:38:51.250 --> 00:38:53.860
So in this particular
case, you do

00:38:53.860 --> 00:38:56.380
need to make sure that
your security model also

00:38:56.380 --> 00:38:58.230
transcends everything.

00:38:58.230 --> 00:39:00.410
Again, where we are
is not somewhere

00:39:00.410 --> 00:39:01.690
you can get to in one step.

00:39:01.690 --> 00:39:03.440
We were cloud native
from the very start.

00:39:03.440 --> 00:39:05.380
We had a shared security
model from the very start.

00:39:05.380 --> 00:39:06.690
We have shared identity model.

00:39:06.690 --> 00:39:08.481
We have a lot of
advantages here at Google.

00:39:08.481 --> 00:39:09.460
We recognize this.

00:39:09.460 --> 00:39:11.710
But you want to get to the
point where your security

00:39:11.710 --> 00:39:15.040
team and your IT team are
on the same page in terms

00:39:15.040 --> 00:39:17.334
of all this kind
of stuff and what's

00:39:17.334 --> 00:39:19.750
going to be necessary for a
given application on any given

00:39:19.750 --> 00:39:20.833
day at any given endpoint.

00:39:20.833 --> 00:39:25.060
And within Google, debate rages
because we have the right tool

00:39:25.060 --> 00:39:26.470
set with the tiered access.

00:39:26.470 --> 00:39:28.960
But there's huge debate
and huge threads which say,

00:39:28.960 --> 00:39:29.860
you know what?

00:39:29.860 --> 00:39:33.880
Is it safe to give this
exact application access now?

00:39:33.880 --> 00:39:35.609
It's always been fully.

00:39:35.609 --> 00:39:37.150
But maybe now we
can get away with it

00:39:37.150 --> 00:39:39.460
in mostly, because we have a new
signal for mostly devices that

00:39:39.460 --> 00:39:40.850
allow us to feel more confident.

00:39:40.850 --> 00:39:43.450
And then security and
IT will debate and talk

00:39:43.450 --> 00:39:44.800
and collaborate for months.

00:39:44.800 --> 00:39:45.520
And then we're
like, you know what?

00:39:45.520 --> 00:39:46.560
Let's give it a try.

00:39:46.560 --> 00:39:48.130
And we'll move its
access, and we'll

00:39:48.130 --> 00:39:50.296
give it to it on a different
tier, and we'll try it.

00:39:50.296 --> 00:39:51.795
So this is a live
strategy, right?

00:39:51.795 --> 00:39:53.920
What I've talked about
today is more of a framework

00:39:53.920 --> 00:39:55.220
for how you get there.

00:39:55.220 --> 00:39:58.240
But putting the right
service in the right tier

00:39:58.240 --> 00:40:00.760
is really important
because, once again,

00:40:00.760 --> 00:40:03.427
otherwise the temptation
is just put it on fully,

00:40:03.427 --> 00:40:04.510
don't trust anything else.

00:40:04.510 --> 00:40:06.176
And you've gone back
to the binary days,

00:40:06.176 --> 00:40:09.930
and you're not going to get
any benefit from the strategy.

00:40:09.930 --> 00:40:12.900
Here, what you'll be able
to do, because you do this,

00:40:12.900 --> 00:40:14.750
is, once again,
deploy services much

00:40:14.750 --> 00:40:18.530
more quickly than you've
been able to before.

00:40:18.530 --> 00:40:19.970
When you're worried
about malware

00:40:19.970 --> 00:40:22.877
and all these kinds of things,
it actually greatly reduces--

00:40:22.877 --> 00:40:24.710
and I gave that example
before for a reason.

00:40:24.710 --> 00:40:26.390
It's actually very
confidence-boosting

00:40:26.390 --> 00:40:28.700
to know that if you see
any threat, no matter what

00:40:28.700 --> 00:40:31.280
the threat is, you can make
adjustments in your cloud

00:40:31.280 --> 00:40:34.820
policy to make sure that
your surface area for attack

00:40:34.820 --> 00:40:37.280
is greatly reduced,
without having to worry

00:40:37.280 --> 00:40:38.710
about your endpoint fleet.

00:40:38.710 --> 00:40:40.700
Because you always have that
issue where you're like, well,

00:40:40.700 --> 00:40:42.050
what percentage of
my fleet is patched?

00:40:42.050 --> 00:40:43.716
What percentage is
running this malware?

00:40:43.716 --> 00:40:44.600
Can we reduce that?

00:40:44.600 --> 00:40:46.844
Well, this person's
traveling, so we kind of--

00:40:46.844 --> 00:40:48.260
What you want to
do is say, great.

00:40:48.260 --> 00:40:50.300
All those things are
just facts of life.

00:40:50.300 --> 00:40:53.060
But what you can actually do
is we can deploy the services

00:40:53.060 --> 00:40:55.520
and put the security and
control in the cloud.

00:40:55.520 --> 00:40:58.550
And by doing that, we
don't have to worry so much

00:40:58.550 --> 00:41:00.260
about the actual
end state posture

00:41:00.260 --> 00:41:02.030
because we know we're
still protected.

00:41:02.030 --> 00:41:04.340
Yes, that individual user
might lose some access

00:41:04.340 --> 00:41:06.020
to some services for a while.

00:41:06.020 --> 00:41:11.030
But that's appropriate
and generally unavoidable.

00:41:11.030 --> 00:41:14.180
You definitely have to
make good hardware choices.

00:41:14.180 --> 00:41:17.681
Like I said before, this is
not about BYOD safe or no.

00:41:17.681 --> 00:41:19.805
And people have come to me
because I'm Android guy,

00:41:19.805 --> 00:41:21.590
and they're like,
Samsung, safe or no?

00:41:21.590 --> 00:41:23.030
How safe compared to LG?

00:41:23.030 --> 00:41:25.730
And I'm like, listen,
there's information

00:41:25.730 --> 00:41:28.214
that can be shared amongst
all of these various things.

00:41:28.214 --> 00:41:30.380
And I'll tell you exactly--
and if you were curious,

00:41:30.380 --> 00:41:32.213
we can talk about exactly
where we've ranked

00:41:32.213 --> 00:41:33.920
various devices here at Google.

00:41:33.920 --> 00:41:36.950
But what we say is there's
a role for every device

00:41:36.950 --> 00:41:38.960
in your actual-- when
you have tiered access,

00:41:38.960 --> 00:41:41.240
there's a role for probably
pretty much every device.

00:41:41.240 --> 00:41:43.734
Because even if they have
very low levels of trust,

00:41:43.734 --> 00:41:45.275
you can give access
to some services.

00:41:45.275 --> 00:41:47.095
It shouldn't be all or nothing.

00:41:47.095 --> 00:41:48.470
And the more
services you can get

00:41:48.470 --> 00:41:50.300
comfortable with
at that basic tier,

00:41:50.300 --> 00:41:52.460
I think the better off
you're going to be.

00:41:52.460 --> 00:41:54.085
But still, for those
higher tier stuff,

00:41:54.085 --> 00:41:56.376
you want to make the right
hardware choices about which

00:41:56.376 --> 00:41:57.570
platforms you support.

00:41:57.570 --> 00:41:59.720
So ultimately this
is all moving towards

00:41:59.720 --> 00:42:01.350
tiered, tiered, tiered, right?

00:42:01.350 --> 00:42:04.100
Like, give yourself
and arm yourself

00:42:04.100 --> 00:42:05.570
with this framework
and the tools

00:42:05.570 --> 00:42:08.450
you need so your
job is just making

00:42:08.450 --> 00:42:11.780
really clever insightful
decisions about where your risk

00:42:11.780 --> 00:42:14.720
profile is and where you're
comfortable taking that risk,

00:42:14.720 --> 00:42:17.390
not with what I see all
the time is people just

00:42:17.390 --> 00:42:21.140
be constrained by that
example I gave before, right?

00:42:21.140 --> 00:42:23.420
BYOD, safe or not safe?

00:42:23.420 --> 00:42:25.970
If you're even thinking
about it in those terms,

00:42:25.970 --> 00:42:29.300
it means you've tied your
own hands in your ability

00:42:29.300 --> 00:42:30.410
to solve this problem.

00:42:30.410 --> 00:42:33.410
And you will be forced to
deploy an imperfect solution

00:42:33.410 --> 00:42:35.120
to the problem,
just by definition.

00:42:35.120 --> 00:42:37.861
You just don't have the tools
you need to actually solve it,

00:42:37.861 --> 00:42:38.360
right?

00:42:38.360 --> 00:42:41.030
It's a hard enough question when
you have the tools you need,

00:42:41.030 --> 00:42:41.230
right?

00:42:41.230 --> 00:42:43.271
Because, again, we debate
all the time about what

00:42:43.271 --> 00:42:45.140
should be on the BYOD
device versus not.

00:42:45.140 --> 00:42:47.150
But if you just
have one big lever

00:42:47.150 --> 00:42:48.920
for access and
not access, you've

00:42:48.920 --> 00:42:52.220
already crippled yourself
in this standpoint.

00:42:52.220 --> 00:42:56.440
So there's a lot of
nuance behind all of this.

00:42:56.440 --> 00:42:59.350
And I don't want to necessarily
say this is a one-stop shop.

00:42:59.350 --> 00:43:02.219
This is, like, a one-hour
session that you're all

00:43:02.219 --> 00:43:03.010
hearing about this.

00:43:03.010 --> 00:43:05.051
And this is years of
thought that we put into it.

00:43:05.051 --> 00:43:07.900
We publish white papers
that you can read about it.

00:43:07.900 --> 00:43:10.360
You can talk to your cloud
reps to hear more about

00:43:10.360 --> 00:43:11.540
how we do it here at Google.

00:43:11.540 --> 00:43:13.510
We have whole things
where our IT department's

00:43:13.510 --> 00:43:15.440
able to share our
learnings, et cetera.

00:43:15.440 --> 00:43:17.830
This is not just a, boom,
we've solved it, easy, buy

00:43:17.830 --> 00:43:19.229
this product, you're all done.

00:43:19.229 --> 00:43:20.770
That's not what I'm
here to tell you.

00:43:20.770 --> 00:43:23.353
What I'm here to tell you is it
is relatively straightforward,

00:43:23.353 --> 00:43:26.180
though, to go and talk about
within your company and say,

00:43:26.180 --> 00:43:29.650
what's our overarching
strategy to endpoint access?

00:43:29.650 --> 00:43:32.230
Do we have the strategy
we need to take us forward

00:43:32.230 --> 00:43:35.770
for the next few years, where
device proliferation is just

00:43:35.770 --> 00:43:37.540
going to be off
the charts, right--

00:43:37.540 --> 00:43:38.380
off the charts.

00:43:38.380 --> 00:43:42.100
Now is the time to be ready
for all the various devices

00:43:42.100 --> 00:43:44.620
and access points that you're
going to have coming forward

00:43:44.620 --> 00:43:46.280
as everything moves
into the cloud

00:43:46.280 --> 00:43:47.860
and as we just
proliferate devices

00:43:47.860 --> 00:43:50.350
to every single part
of the workplace.

00:43:50.350 --> 00:43:52.700
And I have a whole
connected workspaces talk

00:43:52.700 --> 00:43:54.640
that I get which is
not today, but where

00:43:54.640 --> 00:43:57.730
I talk about where we see the
proliferation of devices going

00:43:57.730 --> 00:43:59.850
and coming from.

00:43:59.850 --> 00:44:02.450
So for you, there's
a few links here.

00:44:02.450 --> 00:44:05.380
I apologize for the really
difficult links to type in.

00:44:05.380 --> 00:44:06.380
But you can take a look.

00:44:06.380 --> 00:44:08.254
Definitely google around,
look at BeyondCorp.

00:44:08.254 --> 00:44:10.580
Look at some of our best
practice advisories,

00:44:10.580 --> 00:44:13.100
read more about tiered access.

00:44:13.100 --> 00:44:15.680
And these are areas that you
can do to familiarize yourself

00:44:15.680 --> 00:44:17.750
more, further reading,
further homework

00:44:17.750 --> 00:44:21.334
to see how you adapt this
to your own organization.

00:44:21.334 --> 00:44:23.750
And we, as Google, are obviously
here to help you as well.

00:44:23.750 --> 00:44:24.650
Because I think--

00:44:24.650 --> 00:44:28.490
I will say that there's two
things when I joined Google

00:44:28.490 --> 00:44:29.660
that really blew my mind.

00:44:29.660 --> 00:44:31.410
I'm like, Google just
does so much better.

00:44:31.410 --> 00:44:33.161
I've been here for
three years now, right?

00:44:33.161 --> 00:44:35.576
And I've been in enterprise
mobility and security forever.

00:44:35.576 --> 00:44:37.310
But there's two
products, which honestly,

00:44:37.310 --> 00:44:40.052
as [INAUDIBLE], I'm like, blew
my mind about how good they

00:44:40.052 --> 00:44:41.510
are here at Google,
where I'm like,

00:44:41.510 --> 00:44:44.210
every company needs
to be like this.

00:44:44.210 --> 00:44:45.910
One was video conferencing.

00:44:45.910 --> 00:44:47.410
It has nothing to
do with this talk.

00:44:47.410 --> 00:44:48.868
But I'll just give
you one of them.

00:44:48.868 --> 00:44:51.170
Video conferencing is
really good at Google.

00:44:51.170 --> 00:44:54.260
Every single meeting
has video conferencing.

00:44:54.260 --> 00:44:58.040
Every single meeting is
just if you come in via VC,

00:44:58.040 --> 00:44:59.240
totally part of the culture.

00:44:59.240 --> 00:45:03.157
It takes like three seconds
to set of video conference.

00:45:03.157 --> 00:45:05.240
Even ad hoc 101's are like,
hey, you want to meet?

00:45:05.240 --> 00:45:05.949
Sure, let's meet.

00:45:05.949 --> 00:45:06.531
Let's do a VC.

00:45:06.531 --> 00:45:08.630
It takes me three seconds
to set that up, right?

00:45:08.630 --> 00:45:11.710
Amazingly good at Google,
like blows my mind.

00:45:11.710 --> 00:45:13.460
And the second area
is the approach

00:45:13.460 --> 00:45:15.680
to endpoint security and access.

00:45:15.680 --> 00:45:17.900
I've never seen such
a focus on security,

00:45:17.900 --> 00:45:20.690
because it's Google,
and also such a focus

00:45:20.690 --> 00:45:25.190
on getting as much access
all the time to our employees

00:45:25.190 --> 00:45:26.780
and saying, no, more, more.

00:45:26.780 --> 00:45:29.960
How can we step up
our security story

00:45:29.960 --> 00:45:33.367
so we can give more to people
on lower tiers of access

00:45:33.367 --> 00:45:34.700
while still remaining protected?

00:45:34.700 --> 00:45:36.190
That is just built
into the culture.

00:45:36.190 --> 00:45:37.773
And what that means
is as a Googler is

00:45:37.773 --> 00:45:40.441
that you feel like you can just
focus on getting your job done,

00:45:40.441 --> 00:45:40.940
right?

00:45:40.940 --> 00:45:43.580
Never does my devices
and access really

00:45:43.580 --> 00:45:45.084
feel like it's
getting in my way.

00:45:45.084 --> 00:45:47.000
It just feels like I'm
always able to just get

00:45:47.000 --> 00:45:49.700
stuff done, get stuff done,
which is really my job.

00:45:49.700 --> 00:45:52.040
I really honestly, not
just because I work here,

00:45:52.040 --> 00:45:54.290
feel like there's really
tremendous things that Google

00:45:54.290 --> 00:45:56.350
has done very, very well.

